issues.body:
Delivered in Mura Companion **0.8 — Replicas**.

Completed:
- stable replica/device IDs and common baseline hash;
- append-only validated event model + vector progress;
- deterministic convergence across 2+ replicas;
- explicit conflict records instead of silent last-write-wins;
- replay/idempotency and out-of-order handling;
- LocalSecretStore abstraction with Electron safeStorage where an encrypted backend is actually available;
- encrypted transport-neutral `.mura-sync` packets;
- privacy separation for device-local paths, file-content permissions, timers and window geometry;
- threat-model and real desktop smoke-test documentation;
- 101/101 automated tests passing locally.

Deferred intentionally to 0.9:
- public-key device pairing;
- delta/ack transport;
- optional ciphertext-only relay;
- live visits;
- conflict-resolution UI and log checkpoint/compaction;
- actual macOS/Windows runtime acceptance pass.

Delivered in Mura Companion **0.9 — Paired Transport / Live Visits**.

Completed in code:
- per-device X25519 transport identities and signed pairing ceremony;
- pairing bound to companion identity + replica baseline;
- peer-vector delta-only replica sync;
- AES-256-GCM packets derived from X25519 shared secret via HKDF-SHA256;
- replay protection, sequence tracking and safe out-of-order merge;
- peer revocation, local transport-key rotation and acknowledged-event compaction;
- per-peer + per-packet personal-memory opt-in;
- ciphertext-only blind-relay capsule format;
- signed live-visit invitation/arrival handshake;
- sandboxed click-through guest window with presence/greet/gesture authority only;
- guest receives no file, screen, microphone, host-memory or window-geometry authority;
- 120/120 automated tests pass locally.

Explicitly deferred to 1.0 hardening:
- actual background socket/relay transport client and hosted relay;
- forward-secret online ratchet;
- distributed lost-device revocation across a peer graph;
- richer cross-replica conflict-resolution UI/semantics;
- real macOS/Windows Electron runtime acceptance pass;
- real-time remote animation streaming (0.9 visits are signed presence handshakes).

Next Mura Companion milestone after 0.9 Paired Transport / Live Visits.

Goal: harden Mura into a trustworthy platform foundation before broadening the feature surface.

Planned work:
- run the full Electron acceptance checklist on real macOS + Windows and fix runtime-specific behavior;
- define convergent, user-visible conflict-resolution semantics for non-commutative portable objects;
- build an optional ciphertext-only relay client/server around the existing blind-relay capsule;
- add a forward-secret online session ratchet instead of relying only on the static X25519 relationship key;
- design distributed key rotation / lost-device removal across the peer graph;
- harden log checkpoint/compaction for long-lived identities;
- stabilize the Character Pack SDK and validator so a second first-party character can run on the same engine without Mura-specific assumptions;
- add creator tooling only behind the stable Character Pack contract;
- integrate true source-layer facial art (pupils/eyelids/brows/mouth/hair) when source assets exist;
- keep local-first/offline operation and explicit permission boundaries as release requirements.

Acceptance targets:
1. Real macOS + Windows smoke/acceptance pass completed with documented results.
2. Two or more replicas converge after conflict resolution with deterministic replay tests.
3. Relay compromise exposes ciphertext/transport metadata only, not Mura memory.
4. Online paired sessions provide forward secrecy and reject revoked/lost devices after rotation.
5. A second first-party Character Pack can be installed and validated without engine code forks.
6. Mura remains useful with all networking disabled.

## Why

FOLKOOP v0.28 can now consume real canonical Mura poses instead of painting a fake skin-coloured CSS arm. The current schema-4 Character Pack still has no authored directional pointing set and no true seated/dangling-leg pose.

Do not solve this by renaming `inspect` or `lean-in`. They are useful existing poses but are not the requested body mechanics.

## Required first-party assets

Add canonical Mura assets on the existing **192×208** Character Pack canvas:

- `point-left.webp`
- `point-right.webp`
- `point-up.webp`
- `point-down.webp`
- `sit-edge.webp`

Optional only if visual review proves useful:
- diagonal pointing variants.

## Visual contract

Preserve `mura.default` identity exactly:
- long chestnut-brown hair;
- blue/white striped shirt;
- light-blue cuffed jeans;
- white sneakers;
- existing chibi / semi-cartoon full-body proportions and face.

Pointing poses:
- the hand/finger itself must unambiguously indicate the named direction;
- readable when rendered around **86 px wide** on mobile;
- arm anatomy must belong to the illustration, not a browser overlay;
- enough transparent margin that the fingertip is not clipped.

Seated pose:
- pelvis visibly rests on a horizontal edge;
- knees genuinely bend;
- lower legs hang below the edge;
- shoes remain visible;
- silhouette must still read as seated at mobile helper size;
- no fake “standing sprite shifted downward” acceptance.

## File contract

- VP8X WebP with alpha;
- 192×208 canvas unless a Character Pack schema change is deliberately approved first;
- no opaque/dark matte around the body;
- no external runtime asset dependency;
- update `assets/character/manifest.json` actions;
- Character Pack validator/tests must resolve every new action through the first-party bundle/runtime path.

Suggested actions:
`point-left`, `point-right`, `point-up`, `point-down`, `sit-edge`.

## Acceptance

1. Visual review of all five raw poses at native size and at ~86 px width.
2. Identity comparison against the existing canonical pack.
3. Alpha/canvas contract test.
4. Mura Character Pack validation green.
5. Only after that should FOLKOOP replace its separate direction cue / lean-in surface pose.

This issue is an art/Character-Pack task. It must not be closed merely because CSS or DOM positioning simulates the behavior.
